@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.8}Exercise 3.8:} When we defined the evaluation
model in @ref{1.1.3}, we said that the first step in evaluating an
expression is to evaluate its subexpressions.  But we never specified the order
in which the subexpressions should be evaluated (e.g., left to right or right
to left).  OCaml leaves this order unspecified too, for the arguments of a
procedure call and for operators such as @code{+}.  When we introduce
assignment, the order in which the arguments to a procedure are evaluated can
make a difference to the result.  Define a simple procedure @code{f} such that
evaluating

@example ocaml
f 0 + f 1
@end example

@noindent
will return 0 if
the arguments to @code{+} are evaluated from left to right but will return 1 if
the arguments are evaluated from right to left.
